  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 606 PM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn upper-level trough is moving over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana this afternoon. As a result rain showers will \nlinger across the Hi-Line through this evening with cooler \ntemperatures across the area. On Wednesday an upper-level ridge \nmoves over North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This \nwill bring dry and warm temperatures (near seasonal averages) to the \narea on Wednesday. On Wednesday the pressure gradient increases \nwhich will bring breezy conditions to the Rocky Mountain Front. On \nThursday the upper-level ridge begins to break down. This will keep \nwarmer temperatures with mostly dry weather for the area on \nThursday. Thursday evening a cold front will move through North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. \n\nOn Friday an upper-level trough is over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This combined with the cold front that moved \nthrough will bring cooler temperatures (well-below seasonal \naverages) to the area. On Friday there will be rain showers across \nNorth-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. Saturday through \nMonday there will be an upper-level closed low that will bring \ncooler temperatures and widespread precipitation to the area. Higher \nelevation locations have the potential to receive snow this weekend. \nSee the Forecast Confidence & Scenarios Section for details. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nOn Wednesday there is a 60 - 80% chance for wind gusts of 40 mph or \ngreater. On Thursday there is greater than a 60% chance for wind \ngusts of 40 mph or greater across the Rocky Mountain Front and \nPlains west of 1-15, the Hi-Line, Cascade to Judith Basin County, \nand the North-South orientated valleys of Southwestern Montana. On \nThursday along the Rocky Mountain Front there is a 60 - 80% chance \nfor wind gusts of 55 mph or greater. \n\nSaturday through Monday deterministic and ensemble models differ on \nthe track and strength of the upper-level trough/closed-low. On \nSaturday two clusters have a closed upper-level low in the vicinity \nof Washington/Oregon. This scenario would keep temperatures similar \nto Friday with widespread precipitation and breezy conditions across \nNorth-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. On Saturday one \ncluster has an upper-level trough over eastern Washington and \nOregon. This scenario would keep similar weather to the closed low \nscenario but it would be slightly less windy. A third cluster has a \nupper-level trough over Montana. This scenario would bring cooler \ntemperatures and widespread precipitation across North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. On Sunday three out of four \nclusters have an upper-level trough over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This scenario would bring cool temperatures \nand widespread precipitation to North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. On Sunday the fourth cluster has a closed \nupper-level low off the coast of Washington/Oregon with an upper-\nlevel ridge over Montana. This would bring warm and mostly dry \nweather to North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This \nall shows that this weekend through Monday there is high uncertainty \nin the overall weather pattern. However, in whichever scenario pans\nout there is at least a moderate chance for snow across the \nhigher peaks of North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana \nthis weekend. -IG",
